INT. COLD-STORAGE WAREHOUSE - LOADING DOCK - NIGHT

Frost on the steel. Pallets of frozen fish. A forklift idles. Five figures stand in the glare of a single sodium light.

DETECTIVE LENA VASQUEZ (40s, badge on her belt, calm) holds a pistol low. Behind her, OFFICER CORBIN AMES (20s, vest too big) holds a flashlight. Across the dock, two men in black coats: BLAKE MORROW (50s, smiling) and his silent partner ROURKE GRADY (40s, scar through one eyebrow). And hunched on a pallet, wrapped in a foil blanket, sits a girl of about ten, ASHLEY (10, silent, huge eyes).

VASQUEZ
Hands where I can see them.

BLAKE
Detective Vasquez. We can settle this.

VASQUEZ
(to the girl)
Honey, come over here.

Ashley does not move. Rourke takes a half-step. Vasquez swings the pistol toward him. Corbin's flashlight beam jerks.

CORBIN
Detective?

VASQUEZ
Hold the line, Corbin.

BLAKE
Not with her in the room, I think.

He nods to Rourke. Rourke steps behind the pallet, one hand sliding under his coat. Ashley pulls her knees to her chest.

A DOOR slams at the far end of the dock. A woman in a long raincoat runs in, out of breath, wet hair clinging to her face. This is the WOMAN (30s, hysterical).

WOMAN
Ashley!

The girl looks up. For the first time, she speaks.

ASHLEY
Mama?

Everyone freezes. Blake turns, a smile spreading.

BLAKE
Inez. How kind of you to join us.

VASQUEZ
Ma'am, stay back.

INEZ
That's my daughter.

Rourke draws his gun. Vasquez raises hers. The flashlight beam jerks to Rourke.

BLAKE
Easy. Everybody easy.

VASQUEZ
Rourke, put it down.

ROURKE
(first words, flat)
No.

Inez lunges for her daughter. Rourke turns, gun swinging. A shot rings out. The forklift alarm shrieks.

In the echo, we hold on Ashley's face, calm now, unfrightened, looking at all of them as if she had been waiting.
